  from them or share one with a server.
  [3mCosts:[0m the rule table lives in the shared module, so every new rule kind means editing shared code for one form's
  benefit, plus an escape hatch for anything the table doesn't cover. That's the accretion B avoids.
  [3mComplexity:[0m moderate.

  [1mI recommend B.[0m It's barely larger than A, and unlike A it survives contact with a second form that isn't another
  username/password pair. It beats C on the extension story: custom rules cost nothing and never touch shared code. C's
  readability edge is real but small, and if you later want data-driven schemas, a thin [38;5;153mschemaToRules()[39m adapter sits on
  top of B without changing it — that direction works, the reverse doesn't.

  Two consequences worth naming before you pick:

  - [1mThe result shape changes.[0m Today [38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m returns [38;5;153m{ valid, error }[39m with one string for the whole form.
    Multi-field reuse wants per-field messages, so I'd return [38;5;153m{ valid, errors: { username: "..." } }[39m. That's an
    interface change for [38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m's only caller (the submit handler in [38;5;153mapp.js[39m), which I'd update in the same change.
    Nothing outside this repo calls it.
  - [1m[38;5;153mfile://[39m stops working[0m, as flagged — [38;5;153mindex.html[39m gets [38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m. You'll need a local server ([38;5;153mpython3 -m [39m
    [38;5;153mhttp.server[39m or similar) to open the page.

  I'll leave [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m and [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m alone; that CommonJS pair isn't loaded by the page and converting it isn't
  part of this.
